## Executive Summary

FATF continues to identify the rapid evolution of financial crime risks driven by globalisation, digitalisation, virtual assets, complex ownership structures, sanctions evasion and increasingly sophisticated laundering methodologies.

Recent FATF publications highlight particular concern regarding:

- Virtual assets and stablecoins
- Proliferation financing
- Trade-Based Money Laundering
- Beneficial ownership concealment
- Terrorist financing
- Cross-border value transfer
- Digital identity abuse
- Emerging technology risks

FATF increasingly emphasises risk-based approaches, intelligence-led monitoring and stronger international information sharing to combat evolving financial crime threats.

FATF assessments indicate that financial institutions must move beyond traditional rules-based monitoring toward more adaptive and intelligence-driven detection capabilities.

